const transportReading=`[1] While airships may appear in a few recent novels and films, the reality is that they are few and far between in the modern world. Some can be seen occasionally in the skies above the USA, advertising a product and perhaps carrying a few passengers. A few more are sometimes seen on a tourist outing in parts of Europe.\n\n[2] But if airship enthusiasts are to be believed, in the next few years the enormous Airlander airship will be the first to fly to the North Pole since 1928. Each tourist on board will have to pay $80 000 for this luxury experience, and tickets are already being sold.\n\n[3] The Airlander will accommodate up to 16 passengers and eight crew in the 200-square-metre main cabin. The cabin arrangement can be changed, but the focus is always on comfort and safety — and on offering as many opportunities as possible to see the breathtaking views through the glass windows and floor. The airship can travel up to about 3700 km without stopping, and could stay in the sky for three days at a height of just under 5 km.\n\n[4] At about the same time, an enormous new airship in the shape of a blue whale will take to the skies. This one is not being built for tourism, but for lifting heavy machines and equipment in some of the toughest landscapes on the planet. It is 150 metres long (about the same as an A380 plane) and just as high, made up of 12 storeys. Eventually, these two may not be the only new airships. Nowadays, there seems to be increasing demand for engineers and pilots in an industry that everyone thought was finished.`;
const transportListening=`If you’ve never heard the term before, a boda-boda is a bicycle or motorbike taxi. While these vehicles are common throughout the whole of the African continent, the term boda-boda is specific to East Africa. Here in Uganda, the huge demand for relatively inexpensive public transport, along with cheap imports of motorbikes and the job opportunities for drivers, have all ensured that boda-bodas are here to stay, despite the issue of road safety.\n\nFor many years in Uganda, boda-bodas have been called the country’s silent killers. Boda-bodas, our country’s motorbike taxis, benefit everyone by avoiding traffic jams, navigating unsafe, potholed roads and most crucially providing much-needed employment for young people. They are also injuring and killing thousands every year, monopolising hospital budgets and destroying livelihoods. Since a few hundred appeared on the streets of Uganda in the 1960s, the number of boda-bodas has swelled by tens of thousands every year. In a recent news report, it was estimated that there are hundreds of thousands, and maybe more than 300000 bikes operating in the capital, Kampala.\n\nAs a result, and predictably, the number of motorbike accidents has increased dramatically over the years. According to the Injury Control Centre, there are up to 20 boda-boda-related cases at Mulago National Referral Hospital in Kampala every day, and the strain on the country’s limited health budget is growing. About 40% of trauma cases at the hospital are caused by boda-boda accidents. The treatment of injured passengers and pedestrians accounts for almost two-thirds of the hospital’s annual surgery budget.\n\nOne recent study estimated that 62% of young people in Uganda are jobless. This problem is helped by boda-bodas, as many riders are young people, but the impact of a serious injury can be catastrophic for these riders and their families. Ali Niwamanya, 25, a boda-boda driver for only nine months, was forced to spend three months in Mulago hospital and another five at home recovering after a collision with a car in the capital in September.\n\nWhile the human impact of the boda-boda craze is evident in the packed hospital wards, the strain that road fatalities could have on the economy is worrying politicians. The death toll on Uganda’s roads is twice the average across the rest of Africa. There were more than 3000 road deaths in 2019, but the World Health Organization strongly believes the figure could easily be around 8000.\n\nSome measures are being taken to try to halt the problem. The government announced that more money would be available to improve and maintain roads. The government is establishing a national agency to run campaigns and manage roads. In Kampala, the Capital City Authority is introducing regulations, including first-aid training, reflector jackets and helmets, and a monthly registration fee of 20000 Ugandan shillings paid by the city’s 250000 motorbike taxis.\n\nOther initiatives are also being introduced. The Global Helmet Vaccine Initiative (GHVI) is holding a one-day workshop for 100 riders, part of a national scheme under which 1800 boda-boda riders have been trained in basic road safety. During a study of 12000 boda-boda riders it was observed that only around 4000 drivers and fewer than 100 passengers were wearing helmets. During the workshop the riders learn about overtaking and not carrying more than one passenger, as well as the importance of wearing a helmet and reflector jacket, and carrying their driving licence at all times. On completion of the workshop, each participant receives a yellow helmet bearing the slogan: “Your life is your wealth.”\n\nIn addition, a ridesharing company called SafeBoda now provides safe and efficient boda-boda rides in a number of cities across Africa.\n\nAnd what about the future of the boda-boda with the arrival of e-bikes and scooters? As we know, boda-bodas are generally inefficient and are poorly maintained. As a result, they are highly polluting. But technology will bring the current era of boda-bodas to an end. The introduction of electric boda-boda bikes is expected to reduce air and noise pollution, as well as create more green jobs, and provide a cheaper form of transport.`;
